            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the French for the accident report, then the French for the skid."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: renverser",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "renverser — \"to knock over, to run over; to spill\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Une voiture a renversé un enfant à vélo. A car knocked over a child on a bike."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Also to spill: renverser du café."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "renverser is built on Old French enverser \"to turn upside down\", from envers \"reverse\", which is inherited from Latin inversus, from vertere \"to turn\" (Lewis & Short)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for telling, explaining and describing at B1."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
